    <string name="image_sketch_help_title">How Image Sketch Works</string>
    <string name="image_sketch_help_p1">First the image is converted to grayscale, then inverted (255 minus the gray value). The inverted image is blurred with a Gaussian kernel, and the blurred inverted image is blended with the original grayscale image through the color dodge formula to produce the pencil sketch.</string>
    <string name="image_sketch_help_p2">Core formula: result = min(255, gray × 255 ÷ (255 − blurred + 1e-5)). In flat regions the blurred value is close to the inverted gray, so the division approaches 255 (pure white); along edges the two diverge sharply and the result drops, drawing the dark pencil lines.</string>
    <string name="image_sketch_help_p3">The blur radius controls line weight and retained detail, ranging from 1 to 100 with a default of 15. Smaller radii keep more original detail while larger radii yield cleaner, more abstract lines. To keep the sampling kernel symmetric, even radii are automatically adjusted to the nearest odd value.</string>

